The '''2014 FIFA Club World Cup final''' was the final match of the [[2014 FIFA Club World Cup]], a [[association football|football]] tournament hosted by [[Morocco]]. It was the 11th final of the [[FIFA Club World Cup]], a tournament organised by [[FIFA]] between the winners of the six continental confederations, as well as the host nation's league champions.

The final was contested between Spanish club [[Real Madrid CF|Real Madrid]], representing [[UEFA]] as the reigning champions of the [[UEFA Champions League]], and Argentine club [[San Lorenzo de Almagro|San Lorenzo]], representing [[CONMEBOL]] as the reigning champions of the [[Copa Libertadores]]. It was played at the [[Stade de Marrakech]] in [[Marrakesh]] on 20 December 2014. The Spanish club won the match 2–0 and won their first FIFA Club World Cup title,<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.fifa.com/clubworldcup/matches/round=259701/match=300298898/match-report.html|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141220234228/http://www.fifa.com/clubworldcup/matches/round=259701/match=300298898/match-report.html|url-status=dead|archive-date=20 December 2014|work=FIFA|title=Real Madrid coast to Morocco 2014 title|access-date=20 December 2014}}</ref> and their fourth world club title counting the 1960, 1998 and 2002 Intercontinental Cups, equalling [[A.C. Milan|Milan]]'s record.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.fifa.com/clubworldcup/news/y=2014/m=12/news=real-madrid-turn-their-winning-run-into-a-world-title-2495659.html|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141222031533/http://www.fifa.com//clubworldcup/news/y=2014/m=12/news=real-madrid-turn-their-winning-run-into-a-world-title-2495659.html|url-status=dead|archive-date=22 December 2014|work=FIFA|title=Real Madrid turn winning run into a world title|access-date=21 December 2014|quote=In terms of making history, Real, who have now equalled Milan's record of four World and Intercontinental Cup wins, ...}}<br/>{{cite web|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/025a-0ea6e105e132-ed9adc68e1ac-1000--real-madrid-claim-fifa-club-world-cup/|work=UEFA|title=Real Madrid claim FIFA Club World Cup|access-date=20 December 2014|quote=Madrid equal AC Milan's record of four world club titles, having both also lifted the old European-South American Cup three times before clinching the successor trophy, each under Carlo Ancelotti.}}</ref>

==Background==

===Real Madrid===
[[Real Madrid C.F.|Real Madrid]] qualified for the tournament as winners of the [[2013–14 UEFA Champions League]], following a 4–1 extra time win against [[Atlético Madrid]] in the [[2014 UEFA Champions League Final|final]]. This was Real Madrid's second time competing in the tournament, after finishing fourth in the inaugural tournament in [[2000 FIFA Club World Championship|2000]]. They have played five times in the [[Intercontinental Cup (1960–2004)|Intercontinental Cup]], the predecessor of the FIFA Club World Cup, with three wins ([[1960 Intercontinental Cup|1960]], [[1998 Intercontinental Cup|1998]], [[2002 Intercontinental Cup|2002]]) and two losses ([[1966 Intercontinental Cup|1966]], [[2000 Intercontinental Cup|2000]]). They reached the final after defeating Mexican club [[Cruz Azul]] in the semi-finals.

===San Lorenzo===
[[San Lorenzo de Almagro|San Lorenzo]] qualified for the tournament as winners of the [[2014 Copa Libertadores]], following a 2–1 aggregate win against [[Club Nacional|Nacional]] in the [[2014 Copa Libertadores Finals|final]]. This was San Lorenzo's first time competing in the tournament. They reached the final after defeating New Zealand club [[Auckland City FC|Auckland City]] in the semi-finals.

